[0mAgents share information through a shared global message pool, rather than through one-to-one dialogue. Each agent publishes its structured messages (documents and diagrams that follow a schema and format defined for its role) to the pool. Other agents can then retrieve what they need directly, without asking specific agents and waiting for replies. This keeps the communication topology simple and makes communication more efficient.

To prevent information overload, a subscription mechanism filters what each agent receives. Agents select the information to follow based on their role profiles and interests. In practice, an agent starts its action only after all of its prerequisite dependencies have been received. For example, the Architect mainly follows the PRDs from the Product Manager and pays less attention to documents from roles such as the QA Engineer.

Because the exchanged outputs are structured documents and diagrams, such as the Architect's system interface design and sequence flow diagram, they contain all the necessary information. This avoids irrelevant or missing content and reduces the distortion that can build up over several rounds of conversational communication.
